print("Central limit theorem")
print("Some examples are: ")
print("1. Biology: Biologists apply the CLT when measuring characteristics of organisms. For example, measuring the height of 30 randomly selected plants and calculating the sample mean allows the biologist to estimate the population mean height.")
print("2. Manufacturing: In quality control, managers use the CLT to estimate defect rates. By randomly sampling 60 products and counting defective items, the proportion of defects in the sample can estimate the proportion in the entire production batch. The CLT ensures that the sampling distribution of the proportion is approximately normal, facilitating statistical analysis.")
print("3. Dice or Games: Consider rolling a fair six-sided die multiple times. The population distribution of outcomes is uniform, but if you take repeated samples of two or more rolls and calculate the mean of each sample, the distribution of these sample means will approximate a normal distribution as the number of samples increases.")
print("4. System Performance: In computing, the CLT is used to analyze response times. For example, measuring the latency of a web server over many requests, the average response time across repeated samples will form a normal distribution, even if individual response times are skewed or irregular.")
print("5. Economics: Economists often use the CLT to estimate population averages from sample data. For instance, if an economist randomly selects 50 individuals in a town and calculates their average annual income, the sample mean can be used to estimate the town’s overall average income.")
print("Key Insight: ")
print("The CLT is powerful because it allows statisticians to make inferences about population parameters using sample data, regardless of the original population distribution. It underpins confidence intervals, hypothesis testing, and many practical applications in research and industry.")